.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_RA_ALLOC 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_ra_map_setup ,
.Nm ndi_ra_map_destroy ,
.Nm ndi_ra_alloc ,
.Nm ndi_ra_free
.Nd manage bus resource maps and allocations
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_ra_map_setup
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "char *type"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_ra_map_destroy
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "char *type"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_ra_alloc
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "ndi_ra_request_t *req"
.Fa "uint64_t *basep"
.Fa "uint64_t *lenp"
.Fa "char *type"
.Fa "uint_t flag"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_ra_free
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint64_t base"
.Fa "uint64_t len"
.Fa "char *type"
.Fa "uint_t flag"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip
Device node owning the resource map, or the starting node for lookup.
.It Va type
Null-terminated resource-type string.
.It Va req
Allocation constraints; see \fBndi_ra_request(9S)\fP.
.It Va basep , lenp
Kernel addresses receiving the allocated base and actual length.
.It Va base , len
Base and length of a range to add or return to a free map.
.It Va flag
Zero for a local map, or \fBNDI_RA_PASS\fP to permit ancestor lookup.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The bus resource allocator maintains free ranges indexed by a device node
and a resource-type string.
Predefined types are \fBNDI_RA_TYPE_MEM\fP, \fBNDI_RA_TYPE_IO\fP,
\fBNDI_RA_TYPE_PCI_BUSNUM\fP, \fBNDI_RA_TYPE_PCI_PREFETCH_MEM\fP, and
\fBNDI_RA_TYPE_INTR\fP.
.Pp
\fBndi_ra_map_setup()\fP creates an empty map for \fBdip\fP and \fBtype\fP.
An existing map is left intact.
Add initially available ranges with \fBndi_ra_free()\fP before allocating from
the map.
\fBndi_ra_map_destroy()\fP discards the map and its free-range bookkeeping.
It does not check for outstanding allocations or reclaim resources from
consumers; the caller must finish those uses before destroying the map.
.Pp
\fBndi_ra_alloc()\fP removes a range satisfying \fBreq\fP from the map and returns
its base and length through \fBbasep\fP and \fBlenp\fP.
The request describes size, alignment, optional bounds, an optional fixed
address, and whether partial allocation is acceptable; see
\fBndi_ra_request(9S)\fP.
A partial result consumes resources just like a complete result.
The caller must either use or return that actual range.
.Pp
\fBndi_ra_free()\fP adds the specified range to the free map and coalesces
adjacent ranges.
Use the actual base and length returned by allocation when releasing a range.
Overlapping an already free range is an error.
A zero-length free succeeds without changing a map.
.Pp
With \fBNDI_RA_PASS\fP in \fBflag\fP, allocation and freeing search ancestors if
\fBdip\fP has no map for \fBtype\fP.
The first matching map is used; an exhausted local map does not cause an
allocation request to fall through to an ancestor map.
Use consistent map selection when allocating and freeing.
These functions serialize the allocator's map data, but the caller must
maintain the lifetime of the relevant device nodes.
.Sh CONTEXT
These functions may be called from user or kernel context.
They may sleep.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Map setup returns \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP.
Map destruction returns \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP, or \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP if the map does
not exist.
Allocation returns \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP for a complete allocation,
\fBNDI_RA_PARTIAL_REQ\fP for an accepted partial allocation, and \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP
if no allocation can be made.
Read output base and length only after a complete or partial allocation;
not all failure paths initialize them.
Freeing returns \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP or \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP for a missing map or an
overlapping free range.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Allocate an aligned memory range and return it when finished:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ndi_ra_request_t req = { 0 };
uint64_t base, len;
int ret;
.Pp
req.ra_len = 0x1000;
req.ra_flags = NDI_RA_ALIGN_SIZE;
ret = ndi_ra_alloc(dip, &req, &base, &len,
    NDI_RA_TYPE_MEM, 0);
if (ret == NDI_SUCCESS) {
        /* Use the resource, then release all users of it. */
        ret = ndi_ra_free(dip, base, len, NDI_RA_TYPE_MEM, 0);
        /* Handle a failure to return the range. */
}
.Ed
The map must already contain the ranges made available by the bus.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sunndi 9F ,
.Xr ndi_ra_request 9S
